Subject: [PATCH 26/26] property.fs: encode-int REFUSED what four bytes cannot hold, and (a)'s premise is now derived
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

Two halves of TODO 13.2, and they end differently on purpose.

--- (b) FIXED -----------------------------------------------------------

A 1275 property-encoded integer is FOUR BYTES (5.3.5.1). On a 32-bit cell
that is the whole cell and nothing can be lost. On a 64-bit cell it is
half of one, and `l!-be` wrote the low four bytes and dropped the rest
with no error and no flag. The tree encodes POINTERS through this path
(forth/admin/iocontrol.fs:42,76; forth/device/display.fs:362), so an
ihandle above 4 GiB went into /chosen's `stdin` as its bottom 32 bits and
read back as a different object.

That is the LIED rung of the ladder in CLAUDE.md, and it outranks HALTED
for exactly this reason: an honest failure is debuggable, a false success
is not. `l!-be` now refuses BEFORE the write -- the only place a refusal
is a gate rather than a post-mortem:

    encode-int: value does not fit in the 4 bytes 1275 encodes an
    integer into  Aborted.

WHAT STILL FITS is both halves of the 32-bit range, because both are
reachable and both survive four bytes: top 32 bits all ZERO (an unsigned
quantity -- an address, a phandle, ffffffff) and top 32 bits all ONES
with bit 31 set (a sign-extended negative -- -1 as a full cell). On a
32-bit cell the test short-circuits before any shift, so `20 rshift` is
never executed with a shift count equal to the cell width.

Measured, amd64, one probe run:

    b-ctl-u32-OK                      ffffffff encodes
    b-ctl-neg-OK                      -1 encodes
    b-ENCODING-NOW
    encode-int: value does not fit... 100000000 does not

x86 still reports the input as UNREPRESENTABLE, which is an UNKNOWN and
is printed as one. Nothing in the tree trips the new gate: multiboot,
amd64, amd64-pmem (whose store is at 0x100000000), vga, ppc, diagnostics,
client-forth and dict-identity all pass.

--- (a) NOT FIXED, and this is the measurement that decides it ----------

`l@-be` zero-extends, so the same four bytes decode to -1 on x86 and
ffffffff on amd64, and `-1 encode-int decode-int` is not a round trip at
64 bits.

Sign-extending it is the one-line fix and it is NOT SAFE HERE. It would
turn every decoded value with bit 31 set into a negative cell -- and
`assigned-addresses` carries PCI physical addresses, QEMU's 32-bit PCI
window sits just under 4 GiB, and drivers/vga.fs:148 hands exactly such
an entry to pci-bar>pci-addr through drivers/pci.fs. A framebuffer at
fd000000 would become fffffffffd000000 and be used as an address. The
real fix is signed decode PLUS an `ffffffff and` in every consumer that
wants an unsigned 32-bit address, which reaches drivers/sbus.fs and the
sparc trees this lab cannot boot.

So (a) is tolerated on a PREMISE -- "nothing here decodes a value with
bit 31 set" -- and a claim about a corpus is a cache. Two counters in
l@-be derive it on every boot instead, and property-abi reads them
BEFORE the probe decodes anything of its own:

    a-decodes-boot=2b   a-signbit-boot=0     both arches

with the probe's own deliberate ffffffff as the counter's must-catch
control in the same run (a-signbit-end=1). Without that second reading a
counter wired to nothing would report the same reassuring 0 as a firmware
that never saw one -- which is not hypothetical: the first draft read the
counters at the END, caught the probe's own ffffffff, and that is how the
scoping error was found.

The day a-signbit-boot goes non-zero, (a) has its first real consumer and
the decision is forced. Until then it is measured, not assumed.

diff --git a/forth/device/property.fs b/forth/device/property.fs
--- a/forth/device/property.fs
+++ b/forth/device/property.fs
@@ -13,7 +13,43 @@
   10 here ! here c@ 10 <>
   ;
 
+\ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.2(b).
+\
+\ A 1275 property-encoded integer is FOUR BYTES, by definition (5.3.5.1). On a
+\ 32-bit cell that is the whole cell and nothing can be lost. On a 64-bit cell
+\ it is HALF the cell, and `l!-be` below writes the low four bytes and drops the
+\ rest -- silently, with no error and no truncation flag. The tree encodes
+\ pointers through this path (forth/admin/iocontrol.fs:42,76;
+\ forth/device/display.fs:362), so an ihandle above 4 GiB was written into
+\ /chosen's `stdin` as its bottom 32 bits and read back as a different object.
+\
+\ That is the LIED rung, and it is the one this lab treats as critical: the
+\ record and reality disagree, and nothing errors. Refusing is the HALTED rung
+\ -- not serving, but honestly, with a named reason -- and honest beats false.
+\ So: refuse BEFORE the write, which is the only place a refusal is still a gate
+\ rather than a post-mortem.
+\
+\ WHAT FITS. Both halves of the 32-bit range, because both are reachable and
+\ both round-trip: a value whose top 32 bits are all ZERO (an unsigned 32-bit
+\ quantity -- an address, a phandle, ffffffff) and a value whose top 32 bits are
+\ all ONES with bit 31 set (a sign-extended negative -- -1 as a full cell). What
+\ is refused is everything else, which is precisely the set that cannot survive
+\ four bytes.
+\
+\ On a 32-bit cell the test short-circuits to "fits" before any shift, so the
+\ arch this cannot go wrong on pays nothing -- and `20 rshift` is never executed
+\ with a shift count equal to the cell width, which is where a check like this
+\ would otherwise become undefined.
+: l-fits? ( val -- flag )
+  /n /l = if drop true exit then      \ 32-bit cell: four bytes IS the cell
+  dup 20 rshift                       ( val hi )
+  dup 0= if 2drop true exit then      \ unsigned 32-bit
+  -1 20 rshift <> if drop false exit then
+  80000000 and 0<>                    \ sign-extended negative
+  ;
+
 : l!-be ( val addr )
+  over l-fits? 0= abort" encode-int: value does not fit in the 4 bytes 1275 encodes an integer into"
   3 bounds swap do
     dup ff and i c! 
     8 rshift
@@ -21,10 +57,43 @@
   drop
   ;
 
+\ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.2(a), and it is an INSTRUMENT, not a fix.
+\
+\ `l@-be` accumulates four bytes into a CELL, zero-extending. On a 32-bit cell
+\ four bytes IS the cell and nothing is decided; on a 64-bit cell it is a choice,
+\ and it is the reason the same four bytes decode to -1 on x86 and ffffffff on
+\ amd64. `-1 encode-int decode-int` is therefore not a round trip at 64 bits,
+\ which IEEE 1275 5.3.5.2 says it should be.
+\
+\ THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT SAFE HERE, and that is the finding. Sign-extending the
+\ result makes `-1` round-trip -- and turns every decoded value with bit 31 set
+\ into a negative cell. Those are not rare in principle: `assigned-addresses`
+\ carries PCI physical addresses, QEMU's 32-bit PCI window sits just under 4 GiB,
+\ and drivers/vga.fs:148 hands exactly such an entry to pci-bar>pci-addr through
+\ drivers/pci.fs. A framebuffer at fd000000 would sign-extend to
+\ fffffffffd000000 and be used as an address. The real fix is not one line here;
+\ it is signed decode PLUS an `ffffffff and` in every consumer that wants an
+\ unsigned 32-bit address -- which reaches drivers/sbus.fs and the sparc trees
+\ this lab cannot boot. Patching that blind is how a 64-bit port acquires a
+\ defect nobody can see.
+\
+\ SO MEASURE THE PREMISE INSTEAD. The reason (a) is tolerable today is a claim
+\ about the corpus -- "nothing decodes a value with bit 31 set" -- and a claim
+\ about a corpus is a cache. These two counters derive it on every boot, and the
+\ property-abi track reads them: zero sign-bit decodes means (a) is still
+\ theoretical, and the first non-zero one names the value that ended that. The
+\ count itself is the control: a zero that comes from an instrument nobody ran
+\ reads exactly like a zero that means what it says.
+variable l@be-count    0 l@be-count !
+variable l@be-signbit  0 l@be-signbit !
+variable l@be-last     0 l@be-last !
+
 : l@-be ( addr )
   0 swap 4 bounds do
     i c@ swap 8 << or
   loop
+  1 l@be-count +!
+  dup 80000000 and if 1 l@be-signbit +! dup l@be-last ! then
   ;
 
 \ allocate n bytes for device tree information
